sam: quick check — running into partial JSON when consuming SSE with our Node SDK. stream chunks cut a JSON object in half so JSON.parse throws. example incoming bytes: 

`data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"content":"hel`
`lo"}}]}

data: [DONE]

`

Anyone handled this cleanly? want to avoid full-stream blocking. :thinking_face:

jin: yup seen that. SSE boundaries ("\n\n") only mark event end, but TCP chunks can split lines. you need to reassemble event lines first, then parse event.data. basically buffer until you hit the blank-line separator. :+1:

noah: if the generator itself is sending partial JSON across separate `data:` lines (e.g., `data: {"a":"par` then `data: tial"}`) you should join all `data:` lines for that event before parsing. if still failing, use an incremental json parser like `stream-json` or `clarinet`.

kate: for backpressure, pause the incoming socket while your buffer exceeds N bytes and resume once drained. in Node: `req.pause()` / `req.resume()` or use a Transform with `this.push()` control. also consider trimming older buffered tokens if the client can tolerate lossy drops.

sam: example naive reassembly i tried — still brittle when parse fails mid-event. any small snippet recommendations?

jin: here's a simple approach that waits for event delimiter and only attempts parse when data looks balanced (quick brace check) — keeps it light without a full SAX parser:

```js
let buf = '';
req.on('data', chunk => {
  buf += chunk.toString();
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
    const evt = buf.slice(0, idx);
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 2);
    const dataLines = evt.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:'));
    const data = dataLines.map(l => l.slice(5)).join('\n');

    // cheap balance check
    const open = (data.match(/\{/g) || []).length;
    const close = (data.match(/\}/g) || []).length;
    if (open !== close) {
      // partial json: push the event back to buffer and wait for more
      buf = evt + '\n\n' + buf;
      break;
    }

    try {
      const obj = JSON.parse(data);
      handle(obj);
    } catch (e) {
      // still partial or malformed: stash and break to wait for more
      buf = evt + '\n\n' + buf;
      break;
    }
  }
});
```

noah: that balance heuristic works for most simple cases and is cheap. for high throughput shapes consider `stream-json` to avoid re-copying buffers.

kate: on the backpressure side — keep an eye on event loop stalls. if you buffer too long, `setImmediate` yields help, or offload parsing to a worker/child process. also add an SSE comment heartbeat from server `: hb\n\n` so proxies don't close idle connections.

sam: thanks. one more — we use keepalive heartbeats but want client-side detection to reconnect within SLO (200ms extra). best pattern?

jin: track lastEventTs and set a timer (heartbeatTimeout = expectedInterval * 3). if no event by then, tear down and reconnect with exp backoff. also emit a metric for reconnects so ops can see regressions.

